es you to do  better in school and then you get  opportunities and then that opens up  whole new world to you and that  motivates do to do better at success now  let's say little pita is also highly  intelligent but she's got that right  brain intelligence bummer  and she also has low EQ how she gonna  develop it how's that story has that  story look she's struggling in school no  one's telling her she's smart no one's  telling her that she is this bright  future that's just within her reach now  I mentioned before that the story of  PETA and sadie is very much the story of  my sister and I and I'm now going to  tell you a little bit more about my  sister so you can understand and  visualize kind of how that story with  PETA ends up developing so my sister and  I are Irish twins exactly you're apart  same parents same environment same  everything up until a certain point but  different talents my sister is a  creative genius she's also a human gps  navigator she thinks in shapes and  diagrams whereas i always need  instructions but she didn't excel a  school and what happened to her is  similar to what happens to a lot of kids  that I've encountered in over 20 years  of mentoring she started to make bad  choices she lost hope and then bad  choices led to bad consequences and then  matlin led to worse choices and then  that led to worse consequences now  anyone looking at this story from the  outside would have written her off as  hopeless but along came a mentor  who gave her hope and I literally  witnessed her life do a complete 180 so  my passion for mentoring it didn't just  start with well it did it partly started  with this early sense of injustice  related to education but it also  continues and persists because of the  example of my story of my sister now  what I want to leave you with today is  one hopefully you're now looking at  you're looking at poor performance as  far too nuanced to be explained by  laziness or a no interest on the part of  a student no